The code statements that lead to the bug are:
guard(mutex)(&dev->lock);
...
mutex_destroy(&dev->lock);
kfree(dev);
Reason:
The `guard(mutex)` macro creates an automatic cleanup that calls `mutex_unlock(&dev->lock)` when its scope ends. However, `mutex_destroy()` and `kfree(dev)` are called inside that same scope. When the function returns, the cleanup tries to unlock a mutex that has already been destroyed and whose containing memory has been freed, causing a use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
scoped_guard(mutex, &dev->lock) {
    if (gpio_virtuser_device_is_live(dev))
        gpio_virtuser_device_deactivate(dev);
}
mutex_destroy(&dev->lock);
ida_free(&gpio_virtuser_ida, dev->id);
kfree(dev);
Reason:
The `scoped_guard` limits the mutex lock lifetime to only the block where the activation check is performed. After the block exits, the mutex is unlocked. The subsequent `mutex_destroy()` and `kfree()` are then called outside the locked scope, ensuring the mutex is still valid when being destroyed and preventing any use-after-free during cleanup.

